---
import Footer from "../../components/Footer.astro";
import Photo from "../../components/Photo.astro";
import Video from "../../components/Video.astro";
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Drumsticks"
  section="sticks"
  description="Drumstick sizes (7A, 5A, 5B, 2B), tips, woods and tapers explained, plus brushes, rods and mallets, and how to choose your first pair."
>
  <main>
    <p class="label">Gear</p>
    <h1>Drumsticks</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      What the numbers and letters mean, how tips, woods and tapers change the sound, and how to
      pick your first pair. No brand recommendations: every major maker sells all of these sizes.
    </p>
    <ul class="page-index">
      <li>
        <a href="#anatomy">Parts of a stick</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#sizes">Sizes</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#tips">Tips</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#wood">Wood and materials</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#taper">Taper</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#others">Brushes, rods, mallets</a>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="#choose">Choosing a pair</a>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <section id="anatomy">
      <h2>Parts of a stick</h2>
      <svg
        class="anatomy"
        viewBox="0 0 760 130"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Diagram of a drumstick: butt, shaft, taper, neck and tip"
      >
        <path
          class="stick"
          d="M20 56 Q14 66 20 76 L470 76 C540 76 600 72 660 70 L690 69.5 Q700 74 712 74 Q732 74 732 66 Q732 58 712 58 Q700 58 690 62.5 L660 62 C600 60 540 56 470 56 Z"
        />
        <line x1="30" y1="40" x2="30" y2="56" />
        <text x="16" y="32">
          Butt
        </text>
        <line x1="250" y1="40" x2="250" y2="56" />
        <text x="232" y="32">
          Shaft
        </text>
        <line x1="560" y1="96" x2="560" y2="73" />
        <text x="530" y="114">
          Taper (shoulder)
        </text>
        <line x1="675" y1="40" x2="675" y2="62" />
        <text x="656" y="32">
          Neck
        </text>
        <line x1="718" y1="96" x2="718" y2="74" />
        <text x="706" y="114">
          Tip
        </text>
      </svg>
      <p>
        You hold the stick in the shaft. The <b>taper</b> is where it narrows toward the tip; it
        controls how stiff or flexible the stick feels. The <b>neck</b> is the thin part just before
        the tip, and the part that hits cymbal edges and the hi-hat. The <b>tip</b> shape and
        material shape the sound on cymbals most of all.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="sizes">
      <h2>Sizes: 7A, 5A, 5B, 2B</h2>
      <p>
        The name is a number plus a letter. As a general rule, a{" "}
        <b>lower number means a thicker stick</b>, and <b>B is heavier than A</b>. The letters are
        commonly explained as A for orchestra (lighter, for quieter dance bands), B for band
        (heavier, for louder bands) and S for street (very heavy marching sticks). It's a
        convention, not a standard: the same size can differ a little between brands, so
        measurements below are typical, not exact.
      </p>
      <svg
        class="sizes"
        viewBox="0 0 760 210"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Relative thickness and length of 7A, 8D, 5A, 5B and 2B sticks"
      >
        <text x="0" y="28">
          7A
        </text>
        <rect x="50" y="14" width="620" height="18" rx="9" />
        <text x="0" y="68">
          8D
        </text>
        <rect x="50" y="54" width="650" height="18" rx="9" />
        <text x="0" y="108">
          5A
        </text>
        <rect x="50" y="93" width="650" height="19" rx="9.5" />
        <text x="0" y="148">
          5B
        </text>
        <rect x="50" y="132" width="650" height="20" rx="10" />
        <text x="0" y="190">
          2B
        </text>
        <rect x="50" y="172" width="660" height="22" rx="11" />
      </svg>
      <p class="small">
        Drawn to scale relative to each other, with thickness exaggerated so the differences are
        visible.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Size</th>
              <th>Diameter</th>
              <th>Length</th>
              <th>Feel</th>
              <th>Good for</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>7A</td>
              <td>about 13.7 mm (0.54")</td>
              <td>about 39.4 cm (15.5")</td>
              <td>Light and thin</td>
              <td>Jazz, quiet gigs, smaller hands, fast light playing</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>8D</td>
              <td>about 13.7 mm (0.54")</td>
              <td>about 40.6 cm (16")</td>
              <td>Thin like a 7A but longer, more reach</td>
              <td>Jazz, light rock, players who want a thin stick with more length</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>5A</td>
              <td>about 14.4 mm (0.565")</td>
              <td>about 40.6 cm (16")</td>
              <td>Medium, the all-rounder</td>
              <td>Beginners and most styles: pop, rock, funk, practice</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>5B</td>
              <td>about 15.1 mm (0.595")</td>
              <td>about 40.6 cm (16")</td>
              <td>Thicker and heavier</td>
              <td>Rock, louder playing, larger hands</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>2B</td>
              <td>about 16 mm (0.63")</td>
              <td>about 41 cm (16.25")</td>
              <td>Very thick and heavy</td>
              <td>Heavy rock, metal, building strength on a practice pad</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>
        Heavier sticks are louder and fuller with less effort, but tire you sooner and can overpower
        a quiet room. Lighter sticks are quicker and quieter but can sound thin in a loud band.
      </p>
      <Photo id="sticks" />
    </section>

    <section id="tips">
      <h2>Tips</h2>
      <p>
        The tip touches the cymbal or drum, so its shape changes the sound most on the ride and
        hi-hat. On drums the difference is smaller.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Shape</th>
              <th>Sound</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Acorn</td>
              <td>Full and warm, wide range of tones</td>
              <td>The most common shape; changes sound with the angle you hit at</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Round (ball)</td>
              <td>Clear and focused, the same from every angle</td>
              <td>Popular for jazz and for clean, defined ride patterns</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Barrel</td>
              <td>Broad and punchy, louder on cymbals</td>
              <td>Large contact area, good for rock</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Teardrop and oval</td>
              <td>Warm with good definition</td>
              <td>A middle ground between acorn and round</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Arrow (tapered)</td>
              <td>Bright and articulate</td>
              <td>Cuts through on the ride; the sound changes a lot with angle</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>Wood or nylon tips</h3>
      <p>
        <b>Wood tips</b> sound warmer and darker, especially on cymbals, but they chip and wear over
        time. <b>Nylon tips</b> sound brighter and more "pingy" on cymbals and last longer, but a
        tip can come loose on a cheap pair. Neither is better; try both on a ride cymbal and listen.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="wood">
      <h2>Wood and materials</h2>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Material</th>
              <th>Feel</th>
              <th>Notes</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Hickory</td>
              <td>Medium weight, absorbs shock well</td>
              <td>The most common wood. Durable and comfortable for long sessions</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Maple</td>
              <td>Lighter, quick in the hand</td>
              <td>Good for light, fast playing. Wears out faster under heavy hitting</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Oak</td>
              <td>Dense and heavy</td>
              <td>Very durable and loud, but sends more vibration into your hands</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Synthetic (carbon fibre, aluminium core)</td>
              <td>Varies, usually very consistent</td>
              <td>Last much longer than wood, but feel and rebound differ. Try before buying</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="taper">
      <h2>Taper</h2>
      <p>
        A <b>short taper</b> keeps more wood near the tip: the stick is stiffer, feels more
        powerful, and is more durable. A <b>long taper</b> makes the front of the stick thinner and
        more flexible: more rebound, a lighter feel and a quicker response, but it can break sooner
        when you hit hard. Two sticks with the same size name can feel quite different because of
        the taper.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section id="others">
      <h2>Brushes, rods and mallets</h2>
      <div class="columns">
        <div>
          <h3>Brushes</h3>
          <p>
            Fans of thin wire or nylon. Instead of only hitting, you also sweep them across a coated
            snare head to make a continuous "swish". Essential for jazz ballads, also used in
            country, folk and quiet acoustic sets.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Rods</h3>
          <p>
            Bundles of thin wooden dowels held together. Louder than brushes and quieter than
            sticks, with a softer attack. Great for acoustic gigs and small rooms.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Mallets</h3>
          <p>
            Soft felt or yarn heads. Used for cymbal swells (quick rolls that build up), and deep
            rumbling tom rolls in ballads and film music.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <Photo id="brushes" />
      <Photo id="rods" />
      <Photo id="mallets" />
    </section>

    <section id="choose">
      <h2>Choosing your first pair</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <b>Start with 5A, wood tip, hickory.</b> It suits almost every style and every hand. After
          a few months you'll know if you want thinner or heavier.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Hold several sizes.</b> In a shop, play a few strokes on a pad. The stick should feel
          balanced, not heavy at the front.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Check they're straight.</b> Roll each stick across a flat counter. A warped stick
          wobbles.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Match the pair.</b> Tap each stick on a hard surface. Two sticks with a similar pitch
          have similar weight and density.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>Replace them when they splinter.</b> Chipped wood can cut your hands and damage drum
          heads.
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Watch</h2>
      <div class="columns">
        <Video id="sticks" /> <Video id="brushes" />
      </div>
    </section>

    <Footer />
  </main>
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: 820px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 40px 16px 80px;
  }
  h2 {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--ink);
    padding-top: 18px;
  }
  .small {
    font-size: 15px;
    color: var(--muted);
  }
  svg {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
  }
  .anatomy {
    margin: 16px 0 4px;
  }
  .anatomy text,
  .sizes text {
    font: 15px var(--serif);
    fill: var(--ink);
  }
  .anatomy .stick,
  .sizes rect {
    fill: #e4d3b0;
    stroke: var(--ink);
    stroke-width: 1.2;
  }
  .anatomy line {
    stroke: var(--muted);
    stroke-width: 1;
  }
</style>
